  "notes": "Source: official Collin College TXST Local guided pathway PDF, https://collin.edu/academics/alliance/TXSTComputerScience_9-25.pdf (titled 'TXST Local at Collin College - Guided Pathway: Associate of Science in Computer Science & Information Technology / Bachelor of Science with a major in Computer Science'). Transcribed term-by-term from the PDF text exactly; no courses, credits, or prerequisites invented. This is a TRANSFER PATHWAY, not a standalone Collin degree: years 1-2 are completed at Collin College and years 3-4 are Texas State University courses taught at the Collin College Allen Technical Campus. The final bachelor's degree (BS in Computer Science) is awarded by Texas State University. Only the Collin-taught years 1-2 are transcribed here; the TXST upper-division sequence (years 3-4, e.g. CS 3339, CS 3354, CS 3398, CS 4326, MATH 3398/3305, CS advanced electives, and the 3-hour TXST elective) is deliberately excluded from this record — see the source PDF for that portion. The pathway embeds an Applied Mathematics minor (MATH 2413/2414/2415, MATH 2305, MATH 3398, MATH 3305, CS 3378 per the PDF). CS 3358 Data Structures appears in Second Year Second Semester as listed in the PDF with no stated prerequisite. 'C' in the PDF marks courses fulfilling Texas Core Curriculum requirements. Important notes from the PDF: students must earn a grade of C or higher in all computer science, English, and mathematics courses used for the CS major; PHYS 2425 may not be combined with PHYS 1401 to satisfy core or degree requirements at TXST; a maximum of 8 hours of physics apply to the natural science requirement for the CS major; ENGL 2311 satisfies the 3-hour technical/scientific writing requirement; 9 hours of writing-intensive coursework (CS 2315, CS 3398, CS 4326) are required; students must complete 24 of their last 30 hours with TXST. Credits per term as printed in the PDF: Fall Y1 = 18, Spring Y1 = 17, Fall Y2 = 17, Spring Y2 = 17 (69 total). This pathway map is intended for planning purposes only per the PDF disclaimer; students should meet with an academic advisor before enrolling.",
